WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.850
We built ten CO₂ sensors

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.070
before testing one.

3
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.400
All ten sat on

4
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.380
windowsills, by open windows.

5
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.200
They read fresh air while

6
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.180
pupils inside yawned.

7
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.320
Ten times the work, ten times the mistake.

8
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.920
Today: pilot one sensor, place it

9
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.300
well, and set alerts you can trust.

10
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.730
A pilot is one sensor node,

11
00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.770
tested before you build many.

12
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:33.310
One mistake is cheap to fix; ten are not.

13
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.850
Run it a week or two, and note

14
00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.130
everything that goes wrong.

15
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:39.750
Write the test first.

16
00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:42.450
Outdoors, it should read about

17
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.310
four hundred and twenty ppm.

18
00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.530
It should report all week, with no gaps.

19
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.080
And it should rise as the class

20
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.440
fills, and fall when windows open.

21
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.570
Place it where it measures what you

22
00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.970
mean: the air pupils breathe.

23
00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.860
That's seated head height, a

24
00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.200
metre or two from anyone.

25
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.790
Not by windows, doors or

26
00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:06.550
fans, and not in the sun.

27
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.800
Each sensor has its own rule.

28
00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.370
Tank: on the lid, away

29
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.450
from the inlet pipe.

30
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.600
Soil: at root depth, not on the path.

31
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.500
Thermometer: in shade, in moving air.

32
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.290
Quick check!

33
00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:27.180
The CO₂ sensor sits on my desk, a hand's

34
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.320
width from where I talk all day.

35
00:01:29.870 --> 00:01:30.870
What will it show?

36
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.170
Higher than the room.

37
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.960
Breath out holds about a hundred

38
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.320
times more CO₂ than outdoor air.

39
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.380
Keep the sensor a metre

40
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.400
or two from people.

41
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:43.570
Now the alert.

42
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.990
Below eight hundred ppm, the

43
00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.170
room is well aired.

44
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.020
Above a thousand, open the windows.

45
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.270
Above fifteen hundred, the air is

46
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.490
already poor: open them all now.

47
00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.010
Where you draw the line decides

48
00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.650
which mistakes you make.

49
00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.300
Every alert makes two kinds of mistake.

50
00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.990
A false alarm: the light goes

51
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.970
red, but the air is fine.

52
00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.520
A miss: the air is bad, with no warning.

53
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.300
Lower the line: more false

54
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:17.820
alarms, fewer misses.

55
00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.420
So which mistake costs more?

56
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:24.410
For the tank, a miss means a day

57
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.410
without water: warn early.

58
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.770
In a stuffy room, too many false alarms

59
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.630
and teachers ignore the light.

60
00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:34.190
Test your line on the pilot's data.

61
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:36.180
Your turn.

62
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.960
The tank alert keeps

63
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:39.020
going off for nothing.

64
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.040
A colleague moves the

65
00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:43.220
threshold until it never does.

66
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:44.800
Problem solved?

67
00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.830
No: an alert that never goes off

68
00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.930
misses every real problem.

69
00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.890
Find the balance on real data.

70
00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.300
Remember: pilot one, write the

71
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.160
test, place it well, and choose the

72
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.320
mistakes you can live with.

73
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.000
Try it in class tomorrow:

74
00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.460
a placement hunt.

75
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.800
With one thermometer, pupils take

76
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.240
readings in five places, from the

77
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.760
window to the sunny corner.

78
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:14.970
They compare, and choose the

79
00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.350
best spot with a reason.

80
00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:19.790
Next time: keeping it running and secure.
